 1860: // /**
 1861: //    For a point (x1,y1) inside the element and a line segment (x1,y1)-(x2,y2), find the intersection point 
 1862: //    (xIntersect,yIntersect) and the side (intersectingSide) that it intersects.
 1863: 
 1864: //    Inputs:
 1865: //    @param x1, y1: point inside the element
 1866: //    @param x2, y2: other end of the line segment
 1867: 
 1868: //    Outputs:
 1869: //    @param intersectingSide:  side that intersects the line segment
 1870: //    @param nextElement: element that contains the intersection point
 1871: //    @param xIntersect, yIntersect: intersection point  
 1872: 
 1873: //    The equations for the two segments (the one we are considering and one of the sides of the element) are:
 1874: //    P1(tt1) = (x1,y1) + tt1*(x2-x1, y2-y1)  where 0 <= tt1 <= 1
 1875: //    P2(tt2) = (x3,y3) + tt2*(x4-x3, y4-y3)  where 0 <= tt2 <= 1
 1876: 
 1877: //    If the two segments are parallel, they do not intersect. This is checked by the determinant of the two vectors
 1878: //    (Delta) being zero.
 1879: 
 1880: //    The intersection point happens when:
 1881: //    tt1(x2-x1) - tt2(x4-x3) = x3-x1
 1882: //    tt1(y2-y1) - tt2(y4-y3) = y3-y1
 1883:    
 1884: //    Assumption: the 4 points (including those of the side segment) are different.
 1885: //    The eqs. for the 2 lines are: X=X1+(X2-X1)*tt1 and X=X3+(X4-X3)*tt2.	
 1886: //    Output: false: no intersection or colinear; true: exactly 1 intersection.	
 1887: //    If true, (xIntersect,yIntersect) is the intersection.				
 1888: //    Modified to only check tt2, assuming 0<=tt1<=1 is already assured. 
 1889: // **/
